.TH "OSMIUM-INDEX-TYPES" "5" "1.19.1" "" ""
.SH NAME
osmium\-index\-types \- Index types used to store node locations
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]osmium add\-locations\-to\-ways\f[R] and \f[B]osmium
export\f[R] commands have to keep an index of the node locations in
memory or in a temporary file on disk while doing their work.
There are several different ways this can be done which have different
advantages and disadvantages.
.PP
Use the \f[B]\-\-show\-index\-types/\-I\f[R] option on these commands to
show all available index types.
It depends on your operating system which index types are available.
.PP
Use the \f[B]\-\-index\-type/\-i\f[R] option on these commands to set
the index type to be used.
.PP
The default index type is \f[CR]flex_mem\f[R] which will keep all data
in memory and works for small extracts as well as the whole planet file.
It is the right choice for almost all use cases if you have enough
memory to keep the whole index in memory.
.PP
For the \f[B]osmium export\f[R] command, the special type
\f[CR]none\f[R] is used when reading from files with the node locations
on the ways.
(See \f[B]osmium\-add\-node\-locations\-to\-ways\f[R](1) for how to get
a file like this.)
.PP
You can use one of the file\-based indexes for the node location store
to minimize memory use, but performance will suffer.
In this case use \f[CR]sparse_file_array\f[R] if you have a small or
medium sized extract and \f[CR]dense_file_array\f[R] if you are working
with a full planet or a really large extract.
.PP
When using the file\-based index types (\f[CR]*_file_array\f[R]), add
the filename you want to use for the index after a comma to the index
types like so:
.PP
\f[CR]... \-i dense_file_array,index.dat ...\f[R]
.SH MEMORY USE
It depends on the index type used how much memory is needed:
.IP \(bu 2
For \f[CR]sparse_*_array\f[R] types 16 bytes per node in the input file
are used.
.IP \(bu 2
For \f[CR]dense_*_array\f[R] types 8 bytes times the largest node ID in
the input file are used.
.PP
The \f[CR]*_mem_*\f[R] types use potentially up to twice this amount.
.PP
The \f[CR]*mem*\f[R] and \f[CR]*mmap*\f[R] types store the data in
memory, the \f[CR]*file*\f[R] types in a file on disk.
.PP
The \f[CR]flex_mem\f[R] type automatically switches between something
similar to \f[CR]sparse_mmap_array\f[R] for smaller extracts and
\f[CR]dense_mmap_array\f[R] for larger extracts or the whole planet
file.
.PP
If you specify the \f[B]\-\-verbose/\-v\f[R] option, Osmium will display
how much memory was used for the index.
